\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 NOTE: Similar "demand -> experience -> trust -> actual demand\par
Tailoring presupposes multiple stages. The most important addition is the AI.\par
He will prefer to make high design garments, not everyday ones, however, he starts off at first level, working on everyday garments. He gets a penalty of 100 points in importance for the job if he only resumes to everyday clothes, however, once he decides to give up on basic design, and fully go to second stage, then its 300 points.\par
Player must learn either through books or people about sewing, how it works, what fabrics are needed and models. \par
Wrong clothes sell for less and the trust is hurt. Buy your own fabrics, machine, repair said machine if it breaks down after a number of uses, buy yourself a table for inspection, mannequins for shape. One time buy of scissors , rulers , chalk and other hand sewing tools. One time buy the blocks/models for everyday workwear.\par
There's two stages:\par
1. as said before, were you work with orders, either high end fashion, riskier, or daily ones.\par
2. were you work with guess work, you really need to be aware of your high end fashion clients to be sure of this one. You can go back to previous stage if you feel like it, but you have to be careful with cooldowns.\par
EVERYDAY CLOTHING \par
You hone your experience first in everyday clothes to have more flow of high end fashion orders. \par
FIRST STAGE:\par
You choose the material, if its tight, take measurements or you have them on the order, you decide then to use the correct measurements when asked. Sometimes, customers ask if tight, regular or loose, or the material. \par
>Clothes need to be tight, regular or loose. This depends on fabric, but also the purpose of the wear. Some people will be explicit in their demands, some will simply prefer to let you know this. You can ask them, but some might not be keen to explain you or not know. Some may even tell you wrong fits, ask them if they're sure.\par
Shirts: regular \par
Blouses: regular or loose\par
Tunic: loose\par
Sweater: regular or loose\par
Work shirt: regular\par
Trousers: regular\par
Skirts: regular\par
Shorts: regular\par
Work pants: tight \par
Coats: regular or loose\par
Jacket: tight or regular\par
Wrap dresses: loose or regular\par
Shift dresses: regular\par
Work uniform: regular or tight\par
Work apron: loose\par
Accessories: regular\par
MATERIALS:\par
\b Cotton\par
\b0  - 2 shilling per use\par
- easy to sew\par
- usually regular fit\par
\b Linen \b0\par
- 3 shilling per use \par
- easy to sew. Needs serging or zigzagging. \par
- loose or regular fit\par
\b Flannel \b0\par
- 6 shilling per use\par
- easy to sew\par
- loose or regular fit\par
\b Wool\b0\par
- 6 shilling per use\par
- medium to sew. Requires: trimming and pressing, medium heat + some steam\par
- added slowness when dragging the material through the sewing machine\par
- tight or regular fit\par
\b Lightweight wool\b0\par
- 10 shilling per use\par
- medium to sew. Requires hand basting before any seam, as well pressing, use low hat and some steam. \par
- regular\par
\b Heavy cotton\b0\par
- 4 shilling per use\par
- medium to sew. Requires trimming, serging or zigzaging, pressing (lot of steam and high heat, press a bit more than for wool). \par
- added slowness when dragging material around, it can jump around slightly. Needs stronger needle.\par
- regular fit\par
\b Cotton twill\b0\par
- 6 shilling\par
- medium to sew. Requires trimming, serging or zigzaging, pressing (lot of steam and high heat, press a bit more than for wool)\par
- added slowness when dragging material around, it can jump around slightly\par
- tight or regulat fit\par
\b Canva\b0\par
- 5 shilling per use\par
- medium to sew. Requires trimming, serging or zigzaging.\par
- added slowness when dragging material around, it can jump around slightly. Needs stronger needle.\par
- loose fit\par
\b Denim\b0\par
- 8 shilling per use\par
- medium to sew. Requires trimming, serging or zigzaging.\par
- added slowness when dragging material around, it can jump around slightly. Needs stronger needle.\par
- tight or regular fit\par
\b 3. Activities\par
\b0 EXPERIENCE: 0 to 200, with each clothing piece you do, you gain +1 per order. It can also be gained from being a sewing worker or quality control, with 2 points per day spent working. In the beginning, you only get 6 orders per week, however, above 40 points you get 8 orders per week, while above 80 points you get 10 orders. Anything above 120, and you get 12 orders. You get half the points if you do mistakes, no points if grave mistakes. \par
EXPERIENCE 2: For the exquisite taste, 0 to 200 applies differently. Anything below 100 is not even looked at, anything above is like 2 orders per week, 6 orders for anything above 140, 10 for above 180, and 12 orders if 200. \par
IRONING - you need to learn to make the iron a certain heat level before use, if it uses steam, and how long will you keep it on each side of the hem or seam. \par
>Set steam levels : none, some steam, a lot of steam\par
>Set heat levels: low, medium, high\par
>Keep pressing C for several seconds as you move with the mouse the iron over the fabric \par
- if too much steam or heat, shiny marks or the material distorts, having to restart. If too little of anything or too few held, then it does not work and the process has to be repeated again until right values are met.\par
HAND BASTING - you need to learn to make this as well, once you know, it appears as option: basically you stitch temporarily long temporary seams near the actual seam so the clothes don't slip away while under the machine.\par
>the game will have you choose the intervals at which you make the stitch, and how close will you make the thread to the actual seam. It uses a different color for the thread, it is removed manually with clicks again, after the sewing part\par
TRIMMING - another thing you can learn. You cut through the seam allowance AFTER you SEW, it allows for thick fabric to not stack in corners or other places. \par
>don't trim too close to the seam. Don't trim too far away from the guideline\par
>the fabric is thick, trim. If the seam has corner, like collars or pockets, then trim it. The gameplay is going alongside dotted line, with < > buttons and pressing C\par
NOTCHING AND CLIPPING\par
For the curved, you clip the concave part. Notching is done for the convex, by removing extra material at the edges of each outer edge. All curved seams require the notching+clipping. Clipping, it tells you where to clip, as well for notching. This is for all the clothes.\par
SERGING AND ZIGZAGGING\par
>required when the material can fray, means it needs a zigzag, which is medium  and slower, you need to zig zag past the seam line , closer to the outer edges of seams, to prevent fray. The better option is serging, with a special machine called "overlock machine ". With it, you can do it fast and have no issue afterwards. Better revenue, but you must buy the machine.\par
STRONGER NEEDLE\par
Know when to use it, for what materials, otherwise penalty for misusing it.\par

