\b 4. CLOTHES\b0\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf1\b Shirt\cf0\b0\par
Fabric: cotton, linen, lightweight wool\par
Materials: buttons (4-8\f1 ), interfacing for collar & cuffs\par
Pockets: \f0\lang1033 0-\f1\lang9 2 chest pockets\par
Hand sewing: buttons, buttonholes (if not machine), hem finishing\par
\f0\lang1033 Pattern: regular\par
\cf1\b Blouse\cf0\b0\par
Fabric: cotton, linen, silk, rayon\par
Materials: thread, small buttons (4\f1\endash 10), sometimes elastic\par
Pockets: none\par
Hand sewing: delicate seams, button attachment\par
\f0 Pattern: \par
\b Tunic\b0\par
Fabric: linen, cotton, wool (historical)\par
Materials: thread, sometimes ties or a belt\par
Pockets: usually none\par
Hand sewing: neckline finishing, hem\par
Notes: extremely simple pattern\f1\emdash great beginner item.\f0\par
\b\lang9 Sweater\b0\par
Fabric: knitted wool or cotton (not cut from woven fabric)\par
Materials: yarn, thread for finishing\par
Pockets: rarely\par
Hand sewing: seaming knitted panels together\par
Notes: sweaters are knitted, not sewn from cloth.\par
\b Work shirt\b0\par
Fabric: heavy cotton, denim, flannel\par
Materials: thread, 5\f1\endash 8 buttons, interfacing\par
Pockets: 1\endash 2 reinforced chest pockets\par
Hand sewing: buttons, reinforcement stitches\par
Notes: durability > elegance.\par
\b\f0 Trousers\b0\par
Fabric: cotton twill, wool, denim\par
Materials: thread, zipper or button fly, waistband interfacing, 1\f1\endash 2 buttons\par
Pockets: 2\endash 4 (front + back)\par
Hand sewing: waistband finishing, hemming\par
Notes: more complex than shirts due to fit.\par
\b\f0\lang1033 Skirts\b0\par
Fabric: cotton, wool, linen, blends\par
Materials: thread, zipper or buttons, waistband interfacing\par
Pockets: optional (0\f1\endash 2)\par
Hand sewing: hem, zipper finishing\par
Notes: simple skirts are beginner\f2\_\f0 friendly.\par
\b Shorts\b0\par
Fabric: same as trousers (cotton twill, denim)\par
Materials: thread, zipper or buttons, waistband interfacing\par
Pockets: 2\f1\endash 4\par
Hand sewing: hem, waistband finishing\par
Notes: essentially \ldblquote short trousers.\rdblquote\par
\b\f0 Work pants\b0\par
Fabric: heavy canvas, denim, duck cloth\par
Materials: thread, rivets, heavy buttons, zipper\par
Pockets: 4\f1\endash 6, often reinforced\par
Hand sewing: reinforcement stitches, hemming\par
Notes: durability and reinforcement are key.\par
\b\f0 Coats\b0\par
Fabric: wool, heavy cotton, canvas, lined fabrics\par
Materials: thread, lining fabric, interfacing, 3\f1\endash 8 buttons, sometimes a zipper\par
Pockets: 2\endash 4\par
Hand sewing: lining attachment, buttonholes, hem\par
Notes: complex structure; multiple layers.\par
\b\f0 Jackets\b0\par
Fabric: wool, denim, canvas, leather\par
Materials: thread, lining, zipper or buttons\par
Pockets: 2\f1\endash 4\par
Hand sewing: lining finishing, buttonholes\par
Notes: lighter than coats but still structured.\par
\b\f0\lang9 Wrap dresses\b0\par
Fabric: cotton, linen, jersey, rayon\par
Materials: thread, ties or belt, sometimes a button\par
Pockets: optional (0\f1\endash 2)\par
Hand sewing: hem, neckline finishing\par
Notes: simple construction but requires drape.\par
\b\f0\lang1033 Shift dresses\b0\par
Fabric: cotton, linen, wool blends\par
Materials: thread, zipper (back or side)\par
Pockets: optional (0\f1\endash 2)\par
Hand sewing: hem, zipper finishing\par
Notes: straight silhouette; beginner\f2\_\f0 friendly.\par
\b Work uniform\b0\par
Fabric: durable cotton, poly\f2\_\f0 cotton blends\par
Materials: thread, buttons or zipper, patches\par
Pockets: 2\f1\endash 6 depending on job\par
Hand sewing: patch attachment, reinforcement\par
Notes: functional, standardized\par
\b\f0 Work apron\par
\b0 Fabric: canvas, denim, heavy cotton\par
Materials: thread, straps, metal rings (optional)\par
Pockets: 1\f1\endash 3 large pockets\par
Hand sewing: reinforcement stitches\par
Notes: extremely simple and ideal for early\f2\_\f0 game grinding.\par
\b Gloves\b0\par
Fabric: leather, wool knit, cotton\par
Materials: thread, sometimes lining\par
Pockets: none\par
Hand sewing: finger seams (very delicate)\par
\b Scarves\b0\par
Fabric: wool, cotton, silk\par
Materials: thread (minimal)\par
Pockets: none\par
Hand sewing: hemming edges\par
\b Hats\b0\par
Fabric: wool felt, cotton, straw, canvas\par
Materials: thread, interfacing, sometimes lining\par
Pockets: none\par
Hand sewing: brim finishing, lining\par
\b Bags\b0\par
Fabric: canvas, leather, denim\par
Materials: thread, straps, buckles, rivets\par
Pockets: 1\f1\endash 4\par
Hand sewing: reinforcement, leather stitching\par
\b\f0 5. MISTAKES\b0\par
GRAVE Mistakes: wrong tightness level, wrong material used, wrong sizes when cutting proportions, if seams are not properly sewn (like not just going off by a bit, but by a lot or stopping when not needed, or forgetting to close the seam at the end), multiple mistakes, like 4 mistakes.\par
SMALLER Mistakes: if four, its grave, this can appear when using one of the special activities, or simply during the seaming process, if the error isnt too bad.\f1\par
\par
I personally was thinking something like:\par
1. Getting the right materials, here you learn what fabric is needed for what type of costume, you can buy books or learn from other people on how fabrics are used for what parts\par
2. You have models, either done by you after completing correctly one costume of that type, or if you have a book, here you pick the right model with the order\par
3. You begin to draw on clothes the shapes you want to cut out, then, you begin cutting by pressing C, while with the arrows (<- and ->) move the direction of the scissor. Make sure its relatively within pattern, or you have to restart again. At that point, you ll need new material. \par
4. Once you got all the shapes done, you have to assemble it, you have to sew with the machine all the parts. What is hand sewn will be done afterwards. Same mechanics discussed earlier for mechanical sewing will be applied. Mistakes will force you to retry, grabbing new material and cutting again those shapes. You must sew the pieces in the correct order.\par
Finishing up correctly gives you ready to sell clothing piece!\f0\lang9\par
